fsbrain Changes
===============

Version 1.0.0 -- Subcortical visualization, tracts, connectome, and volume-to-surface projection
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- NEW: white matter tract visualization: `vis.tracts()` draws tractography streamlines, optionally with a semi-transparent brain surface as context (the fsbrain equivalent of `plot_tracts` of the Python package `yabplot`). The streamlines can come from TRK or TCK files, from a directory of such files (e.g. the XTRACT atlas), or from in-memory data. Whole-brain tractograms can be read in subsets, and the bundle colors use the usual `makecmap_options` colormaps. See `examples/tracts`.
- NEW: `read.tract.bundles()` reads one or more tract files, or a whole directory of them, into a named list of bundles. Requires `freesurferformats (>= 1.1.0)` (up from 1.0.1).
- NEW: `download_xtract_tracts()` downloads the XTRACT atlas of the 42 major white matter tracts into the package cache, in one of four levels of detail.
- NEW: connectome visualization: `vis.connectome()` draws a connectivity matrix as lines between region centroids, with spheres as nodes and an optional semi-transparent cortex as context (the fsbrain equivalent of `plot_connectome` of `yabplot`). The node positions are computed by the new `subject.region.centroids()`. See `examples/connectome`.
- NEW: `fs.coloredpaths()`, a renderable class for line segments, which is what makes connectome edges (and tracts) drawable. Supported by the generic renderable plumbing (`apply.transform`, `export()`, colorbar metadata) and by both renderer backends.
- NEW: `unit.icosphere()`, `spheres.mesh()` and `coloredmesh.from.spheres()` create sphere meshes and merge many spheres into a single mesh with per-vertex colors.
- NEW: the scimesh bridge handles line renderables (`coloredpaths_to_scimesh()`, `renderables_to_line_layers()`), can render scenes which contain lines but no mesh, and supports anti-aliasing (2x2 supersampling by default, see `options(fsbrain.scimesh.aa_samples)`).
- NEW: volume to surface projection: `subject.vol2surf()` and `template.vol2surf()` sample a volume (MGH, MGZ or NIfTI1 file, or an in-memory array) at the vertices of the surface of a subject or of a template like `fs_LR_32` or `fsaverage`, the equivalent of the FreeSurfer tool `mri_vol2surf` and of `yabplot.project_vol2surf()`. See `examples/voltosurf`.
- NEW: `download_fs_LR_32_labels()` downloads the fs_LR 32k label files (which define the medial wall) into the package cache, so that `template.vol2surf(..., cortex_only = TRUE)` works for that template.
- NEW: `vis.subcortical.region.values()` visualizes one value per region of an atlas that ships its own surface mesh, e.g. the ENIGMA aseg structures, optionally inside a semi-transparent context mesh. Regions can be hidden by assigning them the value `NaN`. See `examples/subcortical`.
- NEW: the subcortical atlas (8 structures per hemisphere) is now also available for the fsaverage template, see `download_fsaverage_atlases()`.
- NEW: coloredmeshes can carry their own rendering style (new `style` parameter of `coloredmesh.from.color()` and `coloredmeshes.from.color()`, plus the magic value `style='from_mesh'`), which is used whenever a rendering or export function is called with the default style.
- NEW: `apply.transform()` supports additional input types: `fs.surface` meshes, `fs.coloredmesh`/`fs.coloredvoxels` instances, rgl `mesh3d`/`tmesh3d` instances (including their normals) and (hemi-)lists of such objects.
- NEW: `volvis.shells()` visualizes a volume as a set of nested, semi-transparent iso-surface shells, e.g. to show the overall shape and the inner structure of a volume at the same time. The iso-levels, the colors and the transparency can be set explicitly or computed automatically, and the shells can be cut open to look inside. See `examples/volvis_shells`.
- NEW: `vis.volume.clusters()` visualizes the clusters of a volume (e.g. the supra-threshold voxels of a statistical map) inside a *single*, semi-transparent anatomical mesh. Each cluster is rendered as a set of nested, smoothly shaded iso-surface shells which span the range from the cluster boundary to its peak and become more opaque towards the core, so both the extent of a cluster and the location of its maximum are visible at the same time. Positive and negative clusters can be rendered together. See `examples/volume_clusters`.
- NEW: exported `index2ras_tkr()`, the affine matrix that maps 1-based R array indices to surface RAS (the FreeSurfer `vox2ras_tkr()` matrix expects 0-based CRS indices, the two differ by one voxel). This is the correct transform for the meshes returned by `volvis.contour()` and for voxel indices as returned by `which(volume != 0, arr.ind = TRUE)`, see `?index2ras_tkr`.
- FIX: volume data that was rendered *together with a brain surface* was offset by one voxel (1 mm) in each axis, because the FreeSurfer `vox2ras_tkr` matrix was applied to coordinates that are 1-based R array indices. This affected `volvis.voxels()` and the contour mode of `vis.volume.on.surface()` (and the new shell functions), which now use `index2ras_tkr()`. The error was confirmed against FreeSurfer itself (`mri_label2vol`), and there is a regression test now.
- FIX: `apply.transform()` mirrored the meshes it transformed: if the linear part of the affine matrix has a negative determinant (which is the case for the commonly used FreeSurfer `vox2ras_tkr` matrix), the transformation flips the orientation, which reverses the winding of all faces and thus inverts every surface normal. Volume data transformed from voxel space to surface RAS was rendered inside-out, e.g. as dark iso-surfaces with wrong shading in `vis.volume.on.surface(volume_mode='contour')`. The orientation is now restored after the transformation (the vertex order within each face is reversed, and stored normals are transformed with the linear part of the matrix).
- FIX: the exported `Triangles3D.to.coloredmesh()` built the faces from the wrong vertices (the fields `v1`, `v2` and `v3` are `Nx3` matrices, one row per triangle, but were read in blocks of three consecutive rows). Measured on a synthetic sphere, 44 percent of the converted faces were degenerate and the total surface area was 4.3 times too large, so volume contours (e.g. from `volvis.contour()`) rendered with the scimesh backend came out as jagged patches with holes. The rgl backend was not affected.
- FIX: `vis.coloredmeshes()` and friends failed with the error `argument is of length zero` for coloredmeshes with a hemisphere value of `NULL`, and the rgl layout code warned about the "old style of passing coloredmeshes" for lists which mix hemisphere-specific with non-hemisphere-specific meshes, e.g. a semi-transparent cortex together with the volume meshes created by `vis.volume.clusters()`. Both cases are now handled correctly.
- FIX: `find.subjectsdir.of()` (and thus `fsaverage.path()` and everything that resolves a template subject through them) treated an *incomplete* subject directory in the package cache as a usable subject. Since `download_fsaverage_atlases()` only downloads atlas files, `fsaverage.path()` returned a directory without surfaces on machines without FreeSurfer, and every surface access failed. A cached subject directory is now only used if it contains the essential subject files.
- FIX: the fsaverage atlas manifest contained a wrong local file name for the `rh.schaefer200.annot` atlas file (a space instead of a period), so that atlas was incomplete.
- FIX: `collayer.from.morphlike.data()` (used by all `vis.*` functions that render morphometry data) treated a hemisphere without any data as a hemisphere in which all values are `NA`, which added a phantom hemisphere to the color layer and could make rendering fail with the unhelpful error `Parameter 'hemi' can only be NULL if 'hemi_list' has exactly length 1`.
- FIX: `can.plot.colorbar()` now returns `FALSE` for a degenerate data range (all values identical), in addition to missing metadata, instead of letting the export fail with `increasing 'x' and 'y' values expected`. This affects all visualization functions if the data contains only a single distinct value.
- DEV: the test suite no longer deletes the optional data cache of developers (`R CMD check` only), and tests that need a real rgl window skip themselves when rgl's headless NULL device is in use. `README_DEVELOPMENT.md` documents how to avoid the flood of rgl windows during a local test run.


Version 0.8.0 -- Camera-based views, shared rgl/scimesh framing
----------------------------------------------------------------------
This version introduces some breaking changes, marked with `BRK` in the list of changes below. We assume they will only affect very few users of fsbrain, but please read the release notes below carefully to decide whether your code is ready to upgrade to fsbrain v0.8.0.

- CHG + BRK: The rgl backend now orients static views via the camera instead of rotating the mesh geometry (`vis.view()`), reusing the same bounding-sphere framing as the scimesh backend (`bounding_sphere()` in `R/view_framing.R`). Rendered output is pixel-identical to the old mesh-rotation approach for the standard views. Note that previously, in the rare case that you manually added your own objects to the fsbrain scene before rendering (e.g., spheres to mark an anatomical position in an image), you had to use the `rgl_actions` parameter to have them rotated along with the mesh for them to stay in the correct spot for different views. This is now no longer needed, and code that does the manual rotation will need to be changed (simple remove manual rotation).
- FIX + BRK: rgl `rostral` and `caudal` views were anatomically swapped (rgl "rostral" showed the posterior/caudal surface and vice versa). They now look from +y (anterior/rostral) and -y (posterior/caudal) respectively, matching FreeSurfer RAS and the scimesh backend. BREAKING for any figure using the rostral/caudal views. Basically the position of these two views in the rare arranged views that use them (only "t9") will swap. If you manually called functions that produced only the caudal or the rostral view, and want the output of v0.8.0 to be identical with earlier versions, you will need to swap the function you call.
- NEW: The scimesh backend now frames views with the same bounding-sphere fit as rgl (radius = half the AABB diagonal, no extra margin) instead of the previous max-extent + 35% margin. This makes rgl and scimesh framing identical. (It was already **very** similar before).
- CHG: Remove dependence on `reshape` and `data.table` packages
- DEV: add regression tests for the view/camera unification (`tests/testthat/test-camera_unification.R`, headless), a shared framing helper (`R/view_framing.R`), and a comparison script for rgl-vs-scimesh orientation (`examples/rgl_vs_scimesh/camera_orientation_compare.R`).
- DEV: add a high-level visual validation script (`examples/rgl_vs_scimesh/validate_rgl_vs_scimesh.R`) that ports the scimesh web examples to plain R and renders each important high-level feature with either backend (`--backend rgl|scimesh`), with optional `--montage` side-by-side output for easy comparison.
- NEW: Add a `scale` parameter to `volvis.slices.with.surface()`, `volvis.lb.with.surface()` and `volvis.lightbox()` that upscales the slice images by an integer factor using nearest-neighbor interpolation, so the MRI pixels stay sharp and blocky (no smoothing, which is what you want for QA) while the images get larger and the overlaid surface contour lines appear relatively thinner. For the surface-contour functions, the contour lines are additionally rendered on a high-resolution overlay that is downsampled to the final size, yielding thin and smooth (anti-aliased) lines — similar to the `freeview` QA view. Defaults to `scale=1L` (no upscaling, so output is unchanged for existing code).
- NEW: The scimesh backend can now render misc3d `Triangles3D` iso-surfaces (e.g., as returned by `volvis.contour()`) in addition to `fs.coloredmesh` instances, matching the rgl backend. Added the exported converter `Triangles3D.to.coloredmesh()` for turning such iso-surfaces into `fs.coloredmesh` instances (useful e.g. for `facecheck`-style volume rendering); the scimesh bridge converts them automatically when they are passed to `export()` / `vislayout.from.coloredmeshes()`, so no manual conversion is needed.
- CHG: `volvis.slices.with.surface()` output filenames now include a `sclN` suffix (the upscaling factor), so exports at different `scale` values no longer overwrite each other.
- FIX: `get.slice.indices()` (used by `volvis.lightbox()` and the volume-slice-with-surface functions) now correctly accepts a numeric vector of explicit slice indices; previously a vector of length > 1 crashed with "the condition has length > 1". Only a single negative value is interpreted as "every nth slice", as documented.


Version 0.7.0 -- Support the scimesh renderer as alternate vis backend
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
- NEW: Support scimesh as an alternate rendering backend to rgl
- - NEW: Add vignette and online notebook documenting the scimesh rendering backend (`vignette("fsbrain_with_scimesh")`)
- FIX: Handle NA vertex colors in the scimesh renderer bridge (treated as fully transparent, like rgl)
- DOC: Add README_MACOS_TAHOE.md with detailed explanation and workarounds for macOS users affected by X11/OpenGL issues.


Version 0.6.0 -- Visualize volume slices with surface contour
-------------------------------------------------------------
- NEW: Add `vis.volume.on.surface()` function that renders a mesh and a volume in the same 3D space simultaneously.
- NEW: Add `volvis.lb.with.surface()` function that renders volume lightbox slices with mesh surface contours overlaid. Supports per-hemisphere surface colors and is great for QA.
- NEW: Add `volvis.slices.with.surface()` function that exports individual volume slices with surface contours as separate image files, ideal for browsing through slices. Supports optional slice index labelling on images and automatic skipping of empty slices. Shares core logic with `volvis.lb.with.surface()` via internal helper.
- FIX: `alphablend()` now preserves computed alpha values in output colors. The old code used `rgb(matrix, alpha=TRUE)` which ignored the alpha column and forced all output to fully opaque. Fixes #28.
- FIX: `annot.outline()` now strips the alpha channel from FreeSurfer annotation colors (which often store alpha=0), so that annotation region outlines remain visible instead of blending to invisibility.
- SEC: Default to https when downloading optional data


Version 0.5.6 -- Minor fixes & maintenance
--------------------------------------------
- Fix all links in the documentation strings to functions from external packages by adding the package to the link
- Update usage of the now deprecated igraph::is.igraph function to the replacement igraph::is_igraph


Version 0.5.5 -- Minor Maintenance
------------------------------------
- Change some minor docstring issues in internal functions (requested by CRAN team)


Version 0.5.4
-------------
- FIX: Change usage of rgl functions that were recently deprectated to current API. This ensures that fsbrain is compatible with the latest rgl version. Thanks to @dmurdoch for the patch. Fixes #52.
- Add 'delete_colorbar_img' parameter to 'combine.colorbar.with.brainview.image()' function. This is TRUE by default, meaning that the merged cbar image file (fsbrain_cbar.png by default) will be deleted after the merge operation by default. This changes the old behaviour, in which the file was left behind in the current working directory.
- Rename some internal functions and fix various small complaints from the latest version of R CMD CHECK, from r-base 4.3. (Required for CRAN upload.)
- NEW: Support plotting colorbar with log scale, implements feature request #44
- FIX: Fix broken hemis on 9 view with inflated surfaces, see issue #50


Version 0.5.3
------------------------
- Add vis.seg.legend() function to visualize legend for a segmented volume based on color lookup table.
- Replace all 'donttest' instructions in examples with 'dontrun', because CRAN runs the 'donttest' stuff and fails on it.


Version 0.5.2
-------------
- Fix: set the example for write.group.morph.standard.sf() to dontrun to prevent creation of files in user home when it is run.


Version 0.5.1
-------------
- Add clip_fun as an alternative to clip.data for rglactions. This makes the API more consistent, see limit_fun.
- More links between function documentation, especially to rglactions. Makes it easier to find navigate the docs.
- Tests: explicitely use testthat namespace in more tests for easier copy and pasting of test code.
- Add some functions for working with FreeSurfer longitudinal preprocessing pipelines and QDEC tables.
- Expose geodesic functions as part of public API.
- Data loading: support aggregating atlas over hemis separately.
- Support drawing region borders with function vis.region.values on.subject
- Docs: more links between docs for easier navigation in help browser, fix various typos.


Version 0.5.0
-------------
- Breaking change: remove long deprecated parameter 'colormap' from all vis functions. Use the 'makecmap_options' parameter instead.
- The function vis.color.on.subject now supports the 'style' parameter supported by other vis functions.
- Add new highlight.* functions which are useful mainly for debugging.
- Fix accidental printing of rglactions to console when calling vis functions.
- Add new 'export' function, an alias for 'vis.export.from.coloredmeshes' so you have to type less. Updated vignettes and example notebooks accordingly.
- Add new entry on vector graphics conversion to the 'fsbrain FAQ' vignette.
- Add surface.curvatures function that can compute k1 and k2 (requires optional dependency package 'Rvcg').
- Add functions to write group standard space per-vertex data: write.group.morph.standard and write.group.morph.standard.mf
- Add new and more convenient volume visualization function: volvis.lb is a convenient wrapper around volvis.lightbox.
- Add new function demographics.to.qdec.table.dat that can convert a demographics dataframe from an Excel or CSV file to qdec.table.dat format and write the additional factor level files.
- Add new sjd.demo() function, a shortcut to download optional demonstration data and get the path to the downloaded subjects dir.
- Add vis.subject.pre function, a new high-level function for visualizing preloaded data (see #31)


Version 0.4.3
-------------
- Add support for exporting figures with transparent background in `vis.export.from.coloredmeshes` function, see parameter 'transparency_color' and docs. A usage example is in the fsbrain FAQ vignette.
- Change default value for the NA-color in 'mkco.*' functions from white (#FFFFFF) to very light gray (#FEFEFE).
- Support custom background color in various visualization functions, including `vis.export.from.coloredmeshes`. See the docs for the new parameter 'background_color'.
- One can now suppress rendering of a colorbar with the `vis.export.from.coloredmeshes` function by passing 'NULL' for the colorbar orientation parameter 'horizontal'.
- Add new R live notebook that illustrates how to use the Export API to produce publication-ready plots. It is linked on the GitHub page, see the Documentation section.
- Disable all unit tests that require extra data on CRAN, downloading them is not possible anymore. We still test on Travis CI and Appveyor CI so that should not make much of a difference.
- Add new 'highlight.vertices.on.subject' function, it does what the name says. Very handy for debugging stuff like coordinate transformations and illustrating things in presentations and bug reports.


Version 0.4.2
-------------
- Fix bug in makecmap_options 'range' handling, see #19 and #20
- Improve documentation for makecmap_options 'range' handling: new entry in fsbrain FAQ vignette
- Expose 'grid_like' parameter in vis.export.from.coloredmeshes to allow plotting a horizontal strip of images


Version 0.4.1
-------------
- Support for visualizing TRK and TCK files (Fiber track files from Diffusion Tensor Imaging) created in Diffusion Toolkit and MRtrix3, see function 'vis.dti.trk'.
- Delete all unit test data after tests have been run if on CRAN.


Version 0.4.0
-------------
- Support passing a single data vector for both hemis combined to vis.data.on.subject and vis.symmetric.data.on.subject
- Add subject.num.verts function to easily retrieve the vertex counts of the meshes for a subject
- Support new background color layers 'curv_light' and 'sulc_light'
- The function vis.symmetric.data.on.subject defaults to a new colormap.
- The metadata IO function to read subjects files and demographics files no longer have a default for the 'header' parameter. This is to prevent people from accidently skipping the first subject.
- Add functions for reading and writing morph data to and from a single MGH or MGZ file: group.morph.standard.sf and write.group.morph.standard.sf
- Add function for writing FSGD files from demographics table (demographics.to.fsgd.file), and to read subjects list from FSGD files (read.md.subjects.from.fsgd)
- new method to set default resolution, and more consistent usage of defaults: fsbrain.set.default.figsize()
- Add group.surface function to load surfaces for a group of subjects
- Add functions for visualization of groups of subjects at once: vis.group.morph.native, vis.group.morph.standard, vis.group.annot, ...
- Extend advanced live notebook with group level plotting examples
- Better support for limiting the plotted data range
- Add new rendering style 'edges', expose style argument for more vis functions
- Fix bug in coloredmesh.from.morph.standard: custom template subject was ignored
- Add option to download a few files from fsaverage3 under FreeSurfer license. User needs to accept license.


Version 0.3.0
-------------
- Fix reading standard space data from template subjects other than fsaverage
- Add new cbry colormap and related mkco.cluster function, great for visualizing clusters
- Support pushing hemispheres apart to prevent overlapping inflated surfaces, fixes #7
- Remove rotate2D, flip2D, and readable.files functions, they have been moved to the freesurferformats package
- requires freesurferformats version >= 0.1.11 (up from 0.1.10)
- default to using rglot() as rgloptions parameter (instead of empty list) for vis functions, i.e., increase default plot resolution


Version 0.2.1
-------------
- Fix a bug in colorbar drawing that lead to wrong colors (see #6)
- Change defaults in functions for drawing a separate colorbar and combining it with arranged images
- Support combining both horizontal and vertical cbars with a brainview image
- Suggest viridis and RColorBrewer packages for great colors, use them if available


Version 0.2.0
-------------
- Breaking: move rotate3D and flid3D function to freesurferformats. To update your code, replace fsbrain::rotate3D with freesurferformats::rotate3D.
- Support export of coloredmeshes to PLY format
- Add new vignette, a Frequently Askes Questions (FAQ) format
- Add R notebook with code and live examples (web)
- Improve documentation in docstrings
- Improve colormap computation and handling, especially drawing of colorbars
- Remove plyr dependendency, require freesurferformats 0.1.10
- The 'colormap' argument to all vis functions is deprecated, use makecmap_options instead. See the vis function docs for an example.


Version 0.1.0
-------------
- Add many new volume manipulation and rendering functions, e.g., lightbox view in volvis.lightbox
- Support cortex_only parameter also in morph concat functions
- Fix bug #2: cortex_only parameter was ignored by group.agg.morph.native and group.agg.morph.standard
- Add subject.mask function to compute medial wall mask (or other masks) for a subject


Version 0.0.3
-------------
- Add function to visualize arbitrary vertices and label data (vis.labeldata.on.subject) and a function to compute the neighbors of a vertex (mesh.vertex.neighbors)
- The vis.data.on.subject function now accepts a character string for the lh_morphdata and rh_morphdata arguments. If a string, it is treated as a filepath and the data is loaded from that file.
- Add the logical parameter 'cortex_only' to various native space morphometry functions (including subject.morph.native, group.morph.native, group.morph.agg.native na group.multimorph.agg.native). When set to TRUE, this parameter will limit the data to vertices which are part of the cortex (as defined by the label file label//h.cortex.label for the respective subject). This allows one to ignore the data from the medial wall in all of these functions.
- Also add 'cortex_only' parameter for the equivalent standard space functions and the morphometry visualization functions.
- Add more examples to various functions, extend the vignette with latest changes and new functionality.
- Remove unused dependencies
- Add function vislayout.from.coloredmeshes which allows one to create a tight layout visualization from arbitrary angle sets


Version 0.0.2
--------------
- Support rglactions and rgloptions to conveniently increase output resolution and create screenshots
- Scale colormaps over several meshes where appropriate. This ensures that the same color in the left and right hemisphere represents the exact same data value.
- Improve doc strings of many functions
- Add helper function to clip data at percentile (clip.data)
- Require freesurferformats version >= 0.1.4 for surface loading fix, adapt mesh visualization to fix
- Introduce masks
- Add functions to visualize labels (vis.subject.label) and masks (vis.mask.on.subject)
- Tons of work on the vignette


Version 0.0.1
--------------
- First release submitted to CRAN.
